  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>`SaveRequest.ContainerId` no longer exists on the contract — proven by a test that would fail if the property were reinstated, not merely by behaviour.</criterion>
    <criterion>A save carrying no `TargetEntity` writes into the acting user's business-unit container and cannot be steered elsewhere by the request.</criterion>
    <criterion>A save carrying a `TargetEntity` writes into the container derived from THAT record, via task 076's existing derivation — exactly one container-selection model exists in the codebase.</criterion>
    <criterion>The ProcessingJob payload carries the server-derived container id, not a client-supplied one.</criterion>
    <criterion>The response still returns the chosen `driveId` (Q5 — the client needs it).</criterion>
    <criterion>`SpeWriteSinkContainerProvenanceGuardTests` classifies every Office sink as server-derived, and Rule A is green.</criterion>
    <criterion>Client-caller inventory is evidenced by grep, not asserted.</criterion>
    <criterion>Build 0 warnings; publish size reported against the 44.96 MB baseline; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>COMPLETED 2026-08-30. Full write-up: notes/task-085-office-save-container.md.

`SaveRequest.ContainerId` DELETED (tombstoned in place, following the `FolderPath` precedent on the same
type). New `OfficeService.ResolveContainerAsync` derives the container from the record the caller was
already authorized against, via task 076's `RecordContainerResolver` — so the authorization key and the
write destination are ONE value. A secure record with no container of its own is REFUSED
(`ContainerDecisionOutcome.FailClosed`) rather than falling back to the shared default. Derivation runs
BEFORE the ProcessingJob payload is serialized, which is what closes the async worker half: previously a
client-chosen container outlived the request inside a Dataverse row.

⚠️ DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM THIS POML'S OWN CONSTRAINT. The brief said "no record → derive from the
acting user's BU". NOT implemented, because `RecordContainerResolver`'s own contract argues against it:
users sit in the Operations subtree while secure records are owned in Secure Projects, so acting-user
resolution writes a secure record's content into the general Operations container — the exact isolation
failure this project exists to close. The owner's Q1 sanctioned acting-user BU for task 076's three
upload-before-a-record-exists CLIENT paths, a different surface; every shipped add-in path sends a
TargetEntity, so Office save's no-record branch is contract-only and uses the existing server-side
config default (fail-closed when unset). Q5's binding requirement — "accepting one in the request is the
vulnerability" — is fully met, and the response still returns the chosen driveId.

ESCALATION TRIGGER DID NOT FIRE. One scare: `useSaveFlow.ts:62` declares `containerId?: string`, but it
is on `JobResultArtifact` — the RESPONSE shape — appearing exactly once. That is Q5 working as designed.
No client sends it in a request; the quick-save body type enumerates its fields without it.

TESTS ASSERT THE CONTRACT, NOT THE BEHAVIOUR, deliberately: no client ever sent ContainerId, so a
behavioural test over today's callers would have passed throughout the defect's life. The new test
checks for ANY container- or drive-naming property, because the defect is the capability rather than the
spelling — reintroducing it as `DriveId` would be the same hole with a different label. Perturbation:
reinstating the property reddens 2 of 3.

Provenance guard: both Office sinks moved ClientSupplied -> ServerDerivedRecord, owner re-pointed to
"085 (CLOSED)". Original finding text retained inside each entry — it is the clearest statement of why
this guard is keyed on SINKS rather than routes (the client value crossed three frames before reaching
the sink, so no route census could see it).

Verification: build 0 warnings; 3/3 new tests; full BFF suite 10,908 passed / 0 failed / 72 skipped;
ArchTests 144 passed / 6 failed == clean-tree baseline; publish 45.12 MB compressed incl. PDBs (0.00
delta, ceiling 60); no vulnerable packages; no packages added. Placement Justification: no new endpoint,
service, interface, DI registration or package — one existing service gains one already-registered
scoped dependency, and a property is REMOVED from an existing contract.</notes>

  <!-- Original POML notes follow -->
  <notes>Row 9 of the task 083 census. The sharpest single finding of the 2026-08-28 session: a route that IS gated, by a filter that IS correct, authorizing against a field that is NOT the one it writes to.

Two claims about this surface were REFUTED during investigation and must not be reintroduced:

(1) "The Office save path derives folders from the document title." FALSE. `SaveRequest.FolderPath` was client-supplied and always null; no client ever set it. It has since been deleted. The observed document-title folders came from an **unsanitized filename** becoming the upload path verbatim (`"…Add In 8/24/2026"` → folder `…Add In 8` → folder `24` → file `2026`; the trailing `8` is the MONTH). Fixed by task 084.

(2) "`GET /api/spe/audit` can confirm the folder provenance." FALSE. `SpeAuditService` is write-only, the Office path logs nothing, and the table has 0 rows.

Both refutations are recorded because each was a confident inference that survived until someone read the source.</notes>
</task>
